---
import '../../styles/annex.css';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

// The "Open in Colab" button reads the notebook from GitHub. It resolves once
// this notebook is committed to the branch below. Flip to 'main' after merge.
const REPO = 'Garden-AI/almanac';
const NOTEBOOK_BRANCH = 'main';
const NOTEBOOK_PATH = 'notebooks/nanopotential.ipynb';
const colabUrl = `https://colab.research.google.com/github/${REPO}/blob/${NOTEBOOK_BRANCH}/${NOTEBOOK_PATH}`;
const downloadUrl = url('/annex/nanopotential.ipynb');
const plotUrl = url('/annex/nanopotential-md-energy.png');
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Builder's Annex · Garden's Almanac</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="64x64" href={url('/favicon.png')} />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Space+Grotesk:wght@500;700&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="annex">
      <div class="annex-topbar">
        <a href={url('/')}>← Almanac</a>
        <span class="brand"><b>Garden's Almanac</b> · Builder's Annex</span>
        <span>for engineers</span>
      </div>

      <section class="hero">
        <p class="hero-eyebrow">Garden's Almanac · Builder's Annex</p>
        <h1>Matter models, from the serving side.</h1>
        <div class="sig">
<span class="c"># an MLIP is a function of the atoms</span><br />
energy <span class="op">=</span> model(Z, positions, cell)      <span class="c"># one scalar, forward pass</span><br />
forces <span class="op">=</span> <span class="k">-∂</span>energy<span class="k">/∂</span>positions        <span class="a">← every inference is a backward pass</span>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-dek">
          The Almanac explains <em>what</em> these models are. This annex is for the people building
          around them: serving them, speeding them up, fine-tuning them. It assumes PyTorch,
          autograd, and a GPU, and not much chemistry. Transformer ideas get explained where they
          come up. Four pieces, in reading order.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="steps">
        <a class="step" href={url('/annex/the-machine')}>
          <span class="step-num">01 · understand</span>
          <h2>The Machine</h2>
          <p>
            How a pile of atoms becomes an energy and a force, and the five properties of that
            computation that shape everything you serve, speed up, or fine-tune.
          </p>
          <span class="go">read →</span>
        </a>

        <div class="step step--notebook">
          <span class="step-num">02 · run it</span>
          <h2>nanoPotential</h2>
          <img class="plot" src={plotUrl} alt="Energy over a constant-energy MD run: potential and kinetic energy trade in anti-phase while total energy stays flat." />
          <p>
            A ~150-line MLIP in one file of plain PyTorch. It trains, runs molecular dynamics, and
            conserves energy. The plot is a real run. Open it and change things.
          </p>
          <div class="run-actions">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href={colabUrl}>▶ Open in Colab</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={downloadUrl} download>↓ Download .ipynb</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <a class="step" href={url('/annex/the-performance')}>
          <span class="step-num">03 · make it fast</span>
          <h2>Where the Time Goes</h2>
          <p>
            The cost profile of one MLIP call, and the developments that move it: fused kernels,
            compilation, direct-force heads, strict locality. Which one matters depends on the run.
          </p>
          <span class="go">read →</span>
        </a>

        <a class="step" href={url('/annex/the-fine-tuning-problem')}>
          <span class="step-num">04 · adapt it</span>
          <h2>The Fine-Tuning Problem</h2>
          <p>
            Why fine-tuning an MLIP is its own problem: labels on a hidden baseline, second-order
            autograd that fights the fast path, and validation that is dynamical, not a number.
          </p>
          <span class="go">read →</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <p class="hero-dek" style="max-width:64ch;">
        You don't need the main Almanac to read these. Its
        {' '}<a href={url('/primer/')}>primer</a> covers the underlying physics if you want it.
      </p>

      <footer class="annex-foot">
        <p>
          The <a href={url('/')}>Garden's Almanac</a> proper catalogs the models, datasets, and
          clusters. This annex is its engineering companion. Supported by
          {' '}<a href="https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=2209892">NSF Award #2209892</a>.
        </p>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
